  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 204 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nMid-level ridging continues to expand westward across the Southern \nPlains this afternoon. A subtle mid-level wave and isentropic ascent \nhave generated persistent isolated showers across central Kansas, \nbut have seen a decrease in coverage this afternoon and expect that \ntrend to continue. Temperatures warmed up quickly once morning \nclouds/fog burned off with current observations in the low 80s to \nmid 90s and heat indices around 100 degrees along and south of \nInterstate 70. Highest chances for precipitation overnight will be \nnorth of the forecast area along the nose of the low-level jet and \nnear a shortwave lifting northeast. The pressure gradient tightens \nas a surface low moves into Nebraska, keeping the boundary layer \nsomewhat mixed overnight and limiting the fog potential. \n\nSouthwest winds gusting at 20-30 MPH will help create another hot \nday on Friday with highs in the 90s. Dewpoints will mix out to some \ndegree, especially across central Kansas. Even so, heat indices will \nagain rise to around 100 degrees during the afternoon. Short-term \nguidance remains varied in placement of a surface front by Friday \nevening. The majority of guidance keeps the boundary, and associated \nscattered precipitation, northwest of the forecast area. There are a \ncouple of CAMs that do push the boundary close enough to produce \nsome showers or a storm across north-central and far northern Kansas \nFriday evening and night. \n\nA closed low slowly works its way east from the Rockies to the \nPlains Saturday through Monday, pushing a cold front through the \narea. Frontal position by Saturday afternoon will dictate where \nstorms develop and current indications are that the front will \nreside somewhere across central Kansas by mid-day. With an uncapped \nand moderately unstable environment ahead of the boundary, lift \nalong the front should be sufficient for convection to develop. \nShear is progged to be marginal (20-30kts), but high DCAPE values \nsupport the potential for severe downbursts. Details in timing, \nlocation, and convective evolution will become clearer as the system \napproaches. The threat for heavy rainfall and flooding looks low at \nthis point, but PWATs will be seasonably high and could lead to \nefficient rainfall rates. Chances for showers and storms continue \nSunday and Monday as lingering mid-level energy passes overhead \nbefore dry conditions are likely for Tuesday. Cooler temperatures \nbuild in behind the front, with highs generally in the 70s and lows \nin the 50s and 60s.",
